        en: {
            date: "15 September 2026", loc: "Paris", read: "4 min",
            title: "Unlock My Data now measures Android apps itself",
            summary: "Unlock My Data downloads, opens and measures the Android apps in its catalogue: binary size, permissions, trackers, and how all of it changes from one version to the next. 131 apps are covered, and every measurement is published with the digest of the file it came from.",
            chapo: "Exodus Privacy publishes the permissions and trackers of an Android app. Nobody publishes what it weighs, or how that weight moves. So Unlock My Data built its own measurement pipeline: 131 apps downloaded and opened, 612 size readings, 524 version-to-version comparisons. The data is public, and so are the digests.",
            body: [
                { type: "p", lead: true, text: "The pipeline does one thing: it fetches the APK file actually distributed, opens it, and counts. No app is installed, no traffic is intercepted, no account is created. This is static analysis of the binary, of the same kind a security team would run, and it is reproducible: the SHA-256 digest of the measured file is published alongside the result." },
                { type: "h2", text: "What the measurement produces" },
                { type: "p", text: "For each app: the size of the distributed file, the number of declared permissions and how many of them touch sensitive data, the trackers identified, the processor architectures shipped, and the domains the code declares. These readings are kept version after version, which makes it possible to say not only what an app asks for today, but what it has added or dropped since last time." },
                { type: "p", text: "That dimension existed nowhere. A permission granted once is never asked again: it can only be compared. The follow-up now covers 524 steps from one version to the next." },
                { type: "h2", text: "Fourteen gates before naming an app" },
                { type: "p", text: "An automated measurement that names a company has to hold up line by line. Every app therefore passes fourteen checks before it can be cited: is the binary attested by its digest, is the version known, is the measurement less than eight months old, is the downloaded file the app itself or a mere launch stub, does our count agree with Exodus Privacy's. Eleven of those gates are blocking: failing one rules the app out of any release." },
                { type: "p", text: "At the latest run, 99 apps out of 131 clear the blocking gates. The other 32 are not suspicious apps: they are measurements we do not consider solid enough to put to a company. They stay in the data, flagged as such." },
                { type: "stats", items: [
                    { v: "131", l: "apps downloaded and measured" },
                    { v: "612", l: "size readings kept" },
                    { v: "524", l: "version-to-version comparisons" },
                    { v: "99 / 131", l: "measurements citable after quality control" },
                ] },
                { type: "h2", text: "Published, verifiable, reusable" },
                { type: "p", text: "The data is published under public/data/apk-lab: one file per app, a manifest carrying the SHA-256 digest of each, and the aggregate tables under public/data/study. The quality report is published too, gate by gate — failures included. A measurement we do not publish is a measurement nobody can challenge." },
                { type: "quote", text: "We are not asking anyone to take our word for it. The measured file carries its digest, the calculation is documented, the data is online: anyone can redo the work and prove us wrong.", cite: "— The Unlock My Data engineering team" },
                { type: "h2", text: "What this pipeline does not say" },
                { type: "p", text: "Static analysis reads what the binary declares, not what the app does at runtime. A tracker present in the code is not necessarily active; a declared domain is not necessarily contacted. Conversely, code loaded after installation escapes the measurement altogether. The catalogue measured is Unlock My Data's own, built from our priorities: it is not a representative sample of the app store." },
                { type: "p", text: "The studies that build on this data will be published separately, one at a time. This release announces the tool, not its findings." },
            ],
        },
